Catharine extracts a piece of paper from the chest, sits down by it, and draws nine dots in three rows of three. "Do you know how to play dots and boxes?" she asks. "We take turns to draw an edge between two dots. Whoever draws the last edge around one of the four boxes captures that box and writes their initial in it - that's `Z' for you and `C' for me. I'll let you start, but since it's an advantage to start, if we get two boxes apiece then I win. And I warn you, I'm unbeatable."

[Your score has just gone up by one point.]

>examine gnomon
It's the part of a sundial that casts the shadow. Made of metal, there is a long screw on one end.

>examine sundial
You're not quite sure why you never noticed the sundial before. It's a square pedestal of stone, with markings for the hours. The gnomon is missing, and there is a small hole in the centre of the flat surface.

>put gnomon on sundial
You attempt to screw the gnomon into the sundial, but it won't fit. After some examination, you realise that this is because the screw threads of the gnomon and the sundial have opposite chirality.

>ask catherine about gnomon
"The word `chirality' means handedness," says Catharine. "It refers to the sense in which a spiral coils, clockwise or anticlockwise."

>again
"The only thing stopping you is the screw thread on the gnomon," says Catharine. "If it weren't for that, it would be easy."

>again
"Can you find a way to reflect the gnomon?" asks Catharine. "Remember, a reflection in three dimensions is a rotation in four dimensions." She giggles.

>again
"If you can't reflect the screw thread," says Catharaine, "maybe you could find a way to file down the gnomon so it fits?"

>again
"I don't think I should give you any more help with that," says Catharine. "If I gave the game away, it would only spoil your fun!"

