"The game is over," says Catharine. "You scored three boxes to my one. It seems that you won. Well, my. I was sure that Mister Conway said that my symmetry strategy would never fail. Still, we live and learn." She tidies away the finished game.

Catharine rummages around in the chest and extracts a large wooden board on which three pegs are set. Three metal disks, each with a hole in the middle, are threaded onto the left peg. "This puzzle is called the Towers of Hanoi," she says. "The idea is to move the three disks from the left peg to the right peg in only six moves, without putting a disk on top of a smaller one, and only picking up the top disk from a peg. Here, have a go."

