"You can't move a piece any more once it's left the board," says Catharine.

Catharine open the chest and extracts another piece of paper and four small pieces. The paper has a three-by-three grid of squares on it, like a noughts and crosses game. Catharine places two pieces at the left and two pieces at the bottom.

"This is a game called Dodgems," she says. "You have the pieces at the bottom, and I have the pieces at the left. You can move your pieces up, left or right, and I can move mine up, down, or right. Your aim is to get both your pieces off the top of the board before I get mine off the right hand side."

